<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Histadrut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as General Federation of Labour</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Histadrut, the General Federation of Labour, was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> in 1920 as the trade union of Jewish workers in Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ben-gurion">David Ben-Gurion</a> it also ran its own enterprises, including the construction firm Solel Boneh, a bank and a health fund, and became a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a>. It remains Israel's largest trade union federation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jewish institutions built under it carried over into a state: the Hebrew school system of 1919, the <b>Histadrut</b> of 1920, the Technion in 1924 and the Hebrew University of Jerusalem in 1925; the Haganah, the Palmach formed with British help in 1942 and veterans of the Jewish Brigade…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>David Ben-Gurion, secretary general of the <b>Histadrut</b> and leader of the Zionist labour movement, declared the State of Israel; Labor Zionism was also shaped by Nachman Syrkin, Berl Katznelson, Ber Borochov and A. D. Gordon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · People</a></li></ul></section>
